# Data, Provenance, and State Rules

Any fetched, uploaded, extracted, generated, transformed, embedded, predicted, or exported data should preserve provenance where relevant.

## Track Provenance Where Applicable

Track:

- source ID
- source type
- document ID
- section/chunk/span ID
- input artifact ID
- transformation method
- model/tool name
- model/tool version
- prompt/config version if applicable
- timestamp
- confidence/quality score
- license/access status
- review status
- transformation history

## Raw vs Curated Data

Raw data is not training-ready or production-trusted.

Before using data for training/evaluation/analytics, apply:

- validation
- deduplication
- normalization
- license/access checks
- quality scoring
- review status
- dataset versioning

## State Management

For persistent state:

- use proper database/storage abstractions
- avoid hidden local files unless explicitly intended
- avoid storing secrets
- include timestamps
- include status fields
- make background processes idempotent

## Dataset Rules

If dataset-building exists:

- keep raw and curated data separate
- preserve original source
- version datasets
- mark review status
- mark license status
- do not train directly on raw data

## Generated Output Rules

Generated output must be distinguishable from source data.

Do not overwrite source material with generated interpretations.
